{
  "requirement": "The review prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to re-raise the decisions and open choices that the brief lists.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation: the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to re-raise the decisions and open choices the brief lists.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_does_not_reraise_the_brief]` is marked `{test}` for it. It reads the real prompt through `review_prompt()`, which loads `references/review-prompt.md` from the `habeas-requirements` skill. It collapses line wrapping with `collapse` and calls `assert_text_in_order`. That helper panics unless the phrases appear in order.\n\nThe test requires two things:\n1. The prompt names what the brief contains: \"the decisions already made, and the open choices already noted\".\n2. After that, the prompt gives the instruction \"Don't re-raise those\".\n\nThe prompt's paragraph in `review-prompt.md` has the phrases in that order: \"…the decisions already made, and the open choices already noted. Don't re-raise those.\" So the assertions match the requirement's two parts: the brief's decisions and open choices, and the directive not to re-raise them.\n\nThe test checks only that both phrases appear in order, not that they sit next to each other. Even so, a prompt that lost or changed either phrase would fail it. The requirement has no separate modes, boundaries or failure cases. I found no gap.",
